WEBVTT

1
00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:03.100
Two kinds of paramecium can each grow alone.

2
00:00:04.360 --> 00:00:06.780
What changes when they draw from one food supply?

3
00:00:08.100 --> 00:00:11.760
These tiny one-celled living things swim through two clear tubes.

4
00:00:13.060 --> 00:00:15.200
Gold food dots drift through both tubes.

5
00:00:16.580 --> 00:00:19.860
Teal swimmers fill one tube, while coral swimmers fill the other.

6
00:00:22.950 --> 00:00:27.910
A population is every member of one species in one place at one time.

7
00:00:29.250 --> 00:00:32.210
Each tube begins with one population and fresh food.

8
00:00:33.490 --> 00:00:35.730
As food arrives, each population grows.

9
00:00:37.270 --> 00:00:41.750
Soon the bars stop rising, because a steady supply cannot support endless growth.

10
00:00:42.833 --> 00:00:47.773
Competition happens when living things need the same resource and there is not

11
00:00:47.773 --> 00:00:51.173
enough for all. It does not require a fight.

12
00:00:52.413 --> 00:00:55.153
A food dot eaten by one swimmer simply disappears.

13
00:00:56.553 --> 00:00:59.473
That food is no longer available to another swimmer.

14
00:01:00.433 --> 00:01:04.913
Now slide both populations into one tube without adding more food.

15
00:01:06.253 --> 00:01:07.773
Which population will stay common?

16
00:01:09.113 --> 00:01:14.333
Which will fall? Make a prediction from the picture, then watch the real counts

17
00:01:14.333 --> 00:01:15.333
build.

18
00:01:20.650 --> 00:01:23.950
Gause tested that comparison with bacteria as food.

19
00:01:25.250 --> 00:01:29.410
He grew each kind alone, then grew them together under the same conditions.

20
00:01:30.970 --> 00:01:33.130
Teal aurelia stayed common in the shared tube.

21
00:01:34.710 --> 00:01:38.110
Coral caudatum rose at first, then fell to a small population.

22
00:01:39.250 --> 00:01:42.770
The fair comparison is each population against itself.

23
00:01:44.070 --> 00:01:48.810
On the last day shown, sharing had held both kinds back, but caudatum much more.

24
00:01:50.130 --> 00:01:52.970
Caudatum lost a much larger share of its alone count.

25
00:01:54.270 --> 00:01:57.930
The data do not show either kind vanishing by the last day shown.

26
00:01:59.490 --> 00:02:02.730
Coral swimmers are still in the tube, but their count is still falling.

27
00:02:03.883 --> 00:02:05.503
This is quiet competition.

28
00:02:06.823 --> 00:02:09.683
The paramecia do not chase or attack one another.

29
00:02:10.943 --> 00:02:12.623
They use the same limited food.

30
00:02:14.223 --> 00:02:18.763
Members of one species can compete too, because they often need the same resources

31
00:02:18.763 --> 00:02:19.763
in the same place.

32
00:02:20.817 --> 00:02:24.257
The lesson's simulation is simpler than a real tube.

33
00:02:25.537 --> 00:02:30.397
Its food is steady, it has no predators, and one kind is set to use food faster.

34
00:02:31.877 --> 00:02:35.197
Gause also warned that other conditions in the liquid mattered.

35
00:02:36.437 --> 00:02:40.957
The counts support competition, but they cannot prove food was the only cause.

36
00:02:42.067 --> 00:02:43.667
Here is the lesson's rule.

37
00:02:45.347 --> 00:02:50.247
Limited resources can restrict populations; cooperation can help members meet their

38
00:02:50.247 --> 00:02:53.387
needs. This film built the first half.

39
00:02:54.807 --> 00:02:58.707
Limited resources can restrict populations, even without a fight.

40
00:02:59.987 --> 00:03:02.927
The second film asks what working together can change.
